<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>tapto</displayName>
  <description>Settings for the tapto programs: tapto-code, tapto-word and tapto-vnc.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="Centlake">Centlake</string>
      <string id="Tapto">tapto</string>
      <string id="Provider">AI provider</string>
      <string id="Commands">Commands</string>

      <string id="Dialect_Claude">Claude (Anthropic API)</string>
      <string id="Dialect_OpenAI">OpenAI (and OpenAI-compatible servers)</string>
      <string id="Dialect_Gemini">Gemini (Google API)</string>

      <string id="DefaultProvider">Default provider</string>
      <string id="DefaultProvider_Explain">The provider the programs use when none is named on the command line.

This is the name of a provider block: "work" for the block configured by the "Work provider" setting, or claude, openai or gemini for the vendor's public API with the user's own key.

Enabled: users cannot change the default provider. They may still pick another with --provider unless "Allow user-defined providers" is disabled or "Allowed providers" is set.

Not configured: the user's own configuration decides.

Writes the config key "provider".</string>

      <string id="WorkProvider">Work provider</string>
      <string id="WorkProvider_Explain">Configures a provider block named "work": the API shape to speak, the endpoint, the model, and where the API key lives. Set "Default provider" to "work" to make it the one the programs use.

API shape: Claude, OpenAI or Gemini. OpenAI is also the shape most local and gateway servers speak (vLLM, llama.cpp, LiteLLM, Azure OpenAI).

Endpoint URL: required, so that a block this policy defines always says where it sends data. For a gateway or an on-premises server, its base URL, for example https://ai-gateway.example.com/v1. For the vendor's public API: https://api.anthropic.com, https://api.openai.com or https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com.

Model: the model name the endpoint expects. Empty uses the program's default for the API shape.

API key: DO NOT put the key itself here. Group Policy objects are readable by every account in the domain. Put a reference to where the key lives on the user's machine:
    wincred:tapto/work        a Windows Credential Manager generic credential (cmdkey /generic:tapto/work /user:tapto /pass:...)
    env:WORK_AI_KEY           an environment variable
    cmd:some-helper.exe       the first line a command prints
or point the endpoint at a gateway that authenticates the user, and leave this empty.

Reasoning effort: OpenAI shape only; low, medium or high, sent as reasoning_effort. Leave empty to let the server decide.

Each field that is filled in is locked for the user; a field left empty is left to the user.

Writes the config keys work-provider-type, work-provider-url, work-model, work-api-key and work-reasoning-effort.</string>

      <string id="AllowedProviders">Allowed providers</string>
      <string id="AllowedProviders_Explain">The only provider names the programs will use, whether picked with --provider or configured as the default. One name per line, for example "work" or "review".

Enabled: any other provider is refused with a message naming the allowed ones. This takes precedence over "Allow user-defined providers". The endpoint of an allowed provider can then be set only by policy: a "-provider-url" the user wrote is refused, so a permitted block cannot be pointed at a personal account.

Not configured: no restriction.

Writes the config key "allowed-providers".</string>

      <string id="AllowUserProviders">Allow user-defined providers</string>
      <string id="AllowUserProviders_Explain">Whether users may use provider blocks they configured themselves, or the vendors' public APIs with their own keys.

Disabled: only providers this policy defines are usable: the "Default provider", the "Work provider", and any block whose "-provider-type" is set under "Additional settings". Anything else is refused, and so is a "-provider-url" the user wrote for one of these blocks: their endpoints can be set only by policy. Use this to keep company data from being sent to a personal account, and set each block's endpoint URL so the policy says where the data goes.

Enabled or Not configured: users may configure and use providers of their own.

Writes the config key "allow-user-providers".</string>

      <string id="Limits">Request limits and timeouts</string>
      <string id="Limits_Explain">Caps on what one request may cost and how long the programs wait for it.

Maximum output tokens: the most the model may generate per request (default 16000).

Maximum tool iterations: how many tool calls one prompt may trigger before the programs stop and ask (default 200).

Connection timeout: seconds to wait for the endpoint to accept a connection (default 30).

Read timeout: seconds to wait for the whole answer; a slow on-premises model may need many minutes (default 300).

Each value filled in is locked for the user.

Writes the config keys max-output-tokens, max-tool-iterations, connection-timeout and read-timeout.</string>

      <string id="AllowListedCommands">Allow-listed commands</string>
      <string id="AllowListedCommands_Explain">Commands the AI agent may run, for every user: a name and the command line it stands for, for example "build" and "cmake --build build --config Debug". The agent can only pick a name; it never supplies shell text of its own.

The command line may carry placeholders the agent fills in: %1, %2, ... and %* for values, %p1 for a path confined to the project tree. Run "tapto-code --help" and see the README's Commands section for the rules.

Enabled: these commands are available in every project and are listed with the scope "policy". A name set here cannot be redefined by the user; other names may still be added by the user unless "Allow user-defined commands" is disabled.

Not configured: no commands are provided by policy.

Writes the values under the "commands" subkey.</string>

      <string id="AllowUserCommands">Allow user-defined commands</string>
      <string id="AllowUserCommands_Explain">Whether users may allow-list commands of their own with "tapto-code command add" or /add-command.

Disabled: only the commands set under "Allow-listed commands" (and the read-only built-ins: wc, head, tail, cat, ls, tree) can be run. Commands in the user's own stores are ignored and "command add" is refused.

Enabled or Not configured: users may allow-list commands of their own.

Writes the config key "allow-user-commands".</string>

      <string id="AdditionalSettings">Additional settings</string>
      <string id="AdditionalSettings_Explain">Any other configuration key the programs read, as name and value pairs, for keys this template has no dedicated setting for: trace-file, system-prompt, print-cot, or a second provider block (review-provider-type, review-provider-url, review-model, review-api-key).

Each key listed here is locked for the user. The value names are exactly the keys the programs document; run "tapto-code --help" for the list.

Never put an API key value here; see "Work provider" for the references to use instead.</string>
    </stringTable>

    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="DefaultProvider">
        <textBox refId="DefaultProvider_Name">
          <label>Provider name (e.g. work, claude, openai, gemini):</label>
        </textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="WorkProvider">
        <dropdownList refId="WorkProvider_Type" defaultItem="1">API shape:</dropdownList>
        <textBox refId="WorkProvider_Url"><label>Endpoint URL (required; the vendor's public API or your gateway):</label></textBox>
        <textBox refId="WorkProvider_Model"><label>Model:</label></textBox>
        <textBox refId="WorkProvider_ApiKey"><label>API key reference (wincred:, env:, cmd:), never the key:</label></textBox>
        <textBox refId="WorkProvider_ReasoningEffort"><label>Reasoning effort (OpenAI shape only):</label></textBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="AllowedProviders">
        <listBox refId="AllowedProviders_List">Allowed provider names:</listBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="Limits">
        <decimalTextBox refId="Limits_MaxOutputTokens" defaultValue="16000">Maximum output tokens:</decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="Limits_MaxToolIterations" defaultValue="200">Maximum tool iterations:</decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="Limits_ConnectionTimeout" defaultValue="30">Connection timeout (seconds):</decimalTextBox>
        <decimalTextBox refId="Limits_ReadTimeout" defaultValue="300">Read timeout (seconds):</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="AllowListedCommands">
        <listBox refId="AllowListedCommands_List">Commands (name = the command's name, value = its command line):</listBox>
      </presentation>

      <presentation id="AdditionalSettings">
        <listBox refId="AdditionalSettings_List">Settings (name = config key, value = its value):</listBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
